<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf Coast of the United States</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf Coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> is the coastline along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. In the 18th century it was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the strip between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and the Perdido rivers formed part of West Florida. The United States absorbed it in stages after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-the-united-states.jpg" alt="Gulf Coast of the United States" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Derfel73; Wapcaplet; Angr · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_map-Gulf_Coast.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gulf Coast, also called the Gulf South, runs along the shores of the five Gulf States: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. It is a coast of inlets, bays and lagoons cut by many rivers, the largest the Mississippi, and much of it is or was marshland. Barrier islands and peninsulas line the west, among them Padre Island off Texas, 130 miles long; marsh dominates from eastern Texas through Louisiana, and bays and inlets mark the Florida coast. The climate is humid subtropical, and the coast is exposed to hurricanes, floods and severe thunderstorms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before Europeans arrived, the peoples of the coast traded widely: shark and alligator teeth and Gulf shells have been found as far north as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, in the mounds of the Hopewell culture. The first European settlers were mainly French and Spanish. The Louisiana Purchase of 1803, the Adams-Onís Treaty of 1819 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas-revolution">Texas Revolution</a> of 1835-1836 brought the coast into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Sugar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> grown by enslaved people made the region rich, and by the mid-19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> was the fourth largest city in the country and the largest away from the Atlantic seaboard.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> damaged the economy of the South, and the Galveston Hurricane of 1900 struck a city that had the third busiest port in the country. In the 20th century the discovery of oil in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and in the Gulf waters made the coast the centre of the American petrochemical industry, while tourism grew from the 1920s. Hurricane Katrina, which struck on 29 August 2005, left over 1,800 dead, and Hurricane Ike in 2008 killed 112 people and left some 300 missing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The coast's economy rests on energy, petrochemicals, fishing, aerospace, agriculture and tourism, and its marshes are nurseries for the fishing and shrimping industries. Its main cities, from west to east, include Brownsville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a>, Houston, Galveston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biloxi">Biloxi</a>, Mobile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a> and Tampa, and the Port of Houston and the Port of South Louisiana handle some of the largest cargo volumes in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf Coast of the United States” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The strip of <b>Gulf Coast</b> between the Mississippi and Perdido rivers remained Spanish after the Louisiana Purchase, though the United States claimed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-west-florida">Republic of West Florida</a></li></ul></section>
